            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Edy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is an excellent glaze, I have used it for years. The only differences is that I add a little mustard to the glaze."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Blair House"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a wonderful recipe for ham. I did add some ground cloves and dijon mustard to the pineapple juice mixture and it gave the ham a great flavor. I would definitely use this again.",
            "datePublished": "2007-01-05T13:05:01.343Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made this glaze a few times and it never thickens up (since I use a 20oz. can of pineapple), so I had to improvise. Instead I poked holes in the ham, put the cherries and pineapples on with toohtpicks then place the ham in a turkey size oven bag (poke holes in the top) and put it in a glass pan. I cook it for an hour, open the bag, then baste the ham every 15 mins or so. This is the BEST ham I ever had! The bag keeps it super moist and there is plenty of glaze/sauce left over for after slicing. HIGHLY RECOMMENDED!",
            "datePublished": "2008-06-14T11:14:10.050Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "lovingbears2"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I always do this with my ham, but don't need to cook it down. I pour a little ginger ale over ham to wet it, add coating of brown sugar and a little sprinkle of ground cloves, then drip more ginger ale to make it like a thick slurry on the ham. Then pin on the pineapple slices and cherries. It turns into a glaze on its own, basting with drippings after first 60 minutes, then baste every 15 minutes until done. Comes out moist, delicious every time. Sometimes I sprinkle a bit of onion powder and garlic powder onto it, as well, to \" up the ante\"! . Our ham gets rave reviews every time!😉👍",
            "datePublished": "2025-04-18T15:00:40.246Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "freebie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Wonderful and absolutely delicious glaze. Added a pinch of ground cloves and a dash of maraschino cherry juice. Reduced on the stove for about 10 minutes. Used this glaze on a Hatfield boneless ham.",
            "datePublished": "2019-12-26T07:26:06.410Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Casey Goetz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent Glaze! Have used several times basting the ham during cooking. This last time I added some of the maraschino cherry juice to the mixture and everyone loved it.",
            "datePublished": "2019-04-22T23:09:44.500Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "eslovejs"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a great recipe. Can be used on pork or turkey ham. I used the same ingredients but the only thing I did differently was slow boil the ham in the pineapple juice prior to baking it. Drain the ham from the juice and use the same juice for the glaze. This way the ham is baking with the pineapple flavor already in it..... . I also added ground cloves to the glaze. This gives it a burst of flavor.",
            "datePublished": "2011-12-13T14:23:41.497Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kuntrycook"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a very simple yet delicious glaze for ham. I used this at Thanksgiving and my ham came out fantastic. Before baking my ham I did make an additional glaze using maybe 1/2 cup dark corn syrup, 1/4 cup honey, and 1/4 cup brown sugar and I coated the ham with this prior to sticking it in the oven. Then I used the pineapple glaze for basting as the recipe instructs you to do. It was the best ham ever. There weren't a lot of leftovers and we quickly wiped those out by having ham and biscuits for breakfast!!!!",
            "datePublished": "2008-12-06T05:27:46.053Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TatiKuz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "At last minute for Easter dinner, I had to use crushed pineapples and some maple praline syrup. Had no cherries! I purreed the pinapple and cooked it with the brown sugar and syrup, i also added a splash of light syrup frommed canned sweet potato . The glaze was awesome! The ham came out moist and we had a delicious dipping sauce. The pineapple falvor just gives it that extra kick. Everyone enjoyed the ham amd liked the flavor",
            "datePublished": "2011-04-29T15:07:09.637Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "judy2304"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have used this glaze on hams a couple of times, and each time the flavor added to the ham is wonderful. This glaze is very thin, but I have enough to spoon it over the ham a few times. Enough of it sticks to the have to produce a wonderful glaze and flavor. As another reviewer mentioned, I also add a little Dijon mustard to the glaze mix. Since I never have enough pineapple juice from the can of pineapple slices, I always add a little orange juice. I will use this glaze for any hams I make from here on.",
            "datePublished": "2017-03-12T07:59:44.647Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jona Sandau"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easy, beautiful and DELICIOUS! I did this with a pre-cooked pre-spiral sliced ham from Safeway and it turned out fantastic. The spiral sliced hams absorb the glaze nicely. Easiest Christmas entree ever!",
            "datePublished": "2009-12-25T21:14:08.240Z"
